# Realisation before DerivedPath

**DONE.** `nix::DrvOutput` and `nix::Realisation` are bound, and
`Store.query_realisation` produces one. `nix::DerivedPath` is not, and
this file says what it would need.

## The choice

Two real Nix types were next, and only one of them was a binding.

**`nix::Realisation` is composition.** `DrvOutput` is a `Hash` and a
string; `Realisation` is a `DrvOutput`, a `StorePath` and a set of
`Signature`. Every one of those is already a wire value (057), so this
type is the first one that is made ENTIRELY of things this repo
already carries. Nothing new had to be invented, and the declaration
is two classes with one hatched body between them.

**`nix::DerivedPath` is three unsolved questions at once.**

    struct DerivedPath : std::variant<DerivedPathOpaque, DerivedPathBuilt>
    struct DerivedPathBuilt { ref<const SingleDerivedPath> drvPath;
                              OutputsSpec outputs; };
    struct SingleDerivedPath : std::variant<Opaque, SingleDerivedPathBuilt>
    struct SingleDerivedPathBuilt { ref<const SingleDerivedPath> drvPath;
                                    OutputName output; };

1. A SUM type on the wire. Every wire value so far is a product - a
   fixed list of fields - and `_wire_fields` cannot say "one of
   these". `@tree` exists for a recursive value and is what `Value`
   uses, but a Value is a PROXY walked by the RPC layer, not a value
   that crosses as its parts.
2. RECURSION. `SingleDerivedPathBuilt` holds a ref to a
   `SingleDerivedPath`, which is the variant it lives in. A message
   can nest itself in protobuf; a `_from_parts` that rebuilds an
   arbitrary-depth tree is a different kind of body from anything
   here.
3. Rendering needs a STORE. `DerivedPath::to_string` takes a
   `StoreDirConfig &`, so `str(dp)` cannot be an accessor of the
   value - which contradicts how every other wire value prints
   itself.

`OutputsSpec` is a fourth variant inside the second question.

So: Realisation now, and DerivedPath when there is a decision about
sum types on the wire. That decision should be made for its own sake,
not discovered while binding a type.

## What it looks like

    DrvOutput      drv_hash: Hash, output_name: str        constructible
    Realisation    id: DrvOutput, out_path: StorePath,
                   signatures: list[Signature]             produced

    message RealisationMsg {
      DrvOutputMsg id = 1;          // which holds a HashMsg
      StorePathMsg out_path = 2;
      repeated SignatureMsg signatures = 3;
    }

Three levels of message nesting, which is the deepest the wire has
carried, and no declaration says a word about protobuf.

## Two decisions worth the ink

**The binding hands back the KEYED type.** Upstream's
`queryRealisation` answers an `UnkeyedRealisation`, because the key is
the one you just passed. `nix::Realisation` is that plus the key, and
the binding builds one - `nix::Realisation{*found, id}` - so a value
that crosses a wire or sits in a list still knows what it is the
realisation OF. Re-attaching the key is not an invention: it is the
caller's own argument.

**Equality diverges from libstore, deliberately.**
`nix::UnkeyedRealisation` compares on `outPath` ALONE - upstream
writes `GENERATE_CMP(UnkeyedRealisation, me->outPath)` with the
comment "TODO sketchy that it avoids signatures" - and
`nix::Realisation`'s defaulted operators inherit it. So in C++, two of
these differing only in who signed them are equal.

The binding cannot follow that. `__hash__` is derived from the
declared parts and `signatures` is one, so two objects that compared
equal would hash differently - which breaks the one invariant Python
asks of a value. So `compare="parts"`, and
`test_a_realisation_compares_on_its_signatures_where_nix_does_not`
pins it. Perturbation-verified: switching to `compare="cxx"` fails
that test by name.

`DrvOutput` is the opposite case and shows the rule is not a habit -
it defaults both operators over exactly the two members that cross,
so `compare="cxx"` is safe and it uses it.

## None means two things, and nothing separates them

`query_realisation` answers None when the store never realised that
output, AND when `ca-derivations` is off - which is every store this
repo tests, because `LocalStore::queryRealisationUncached` checks the
feature and hands back nothing without consulting a mapping.

A caller who gets None has learnt that this store cannot tell them,
which is a different fact from the output not existing. Nothing in the
API separates the two and the declaration says so rather than
implying otherwise.

## Gates, all watched to fail

- `_from_parts` dropping `signatures` - the round trip fails naming
  it.
- `compare="cxx"` on Realisation - the divergence test fails with
  "the binding compares signatures; libstore does not".
- `query_realisation` throwing - the None test fails with the thrown
  error, which proves it exercises the call rather than a default.

## The remote test says what it cannot prove

A wire value has crossed as an ARGUMENT since `is_valid_path` took a
StorePath, and bytes since `add_to_store` took a file's contents. What
is new is a message argument that CONTAINS a message.

The test proves the RPC layer builds and accepts that nesting. It does
NOT prove the digest arrived intact, because the answer does not
depend on it - a client that mangled the digest would still get None,
and nothing bound today echoes a value back to ask. The docstring says
so; the round-trip gate is where fidelity lives.

## Not done

`Store.register_drv_output` takes a `Realisation` and would earn it a
constructor. Nothing binds it, so `Realisation` stays produced-only
and its `_from_parts` is the only way to build one - which is why the
round-trip test needed `_rebuild`, a one-line helper that reaches past
the public surface where a typechecker cannot follow.
